<h2>The Claim Timeline After a Storm</h2>
<p>For many insurers, Texas Insurance Code <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chapter 542</a> sets the general pace of a claim:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Within 15 days of notice,</strong> the insurer generally must acknowledge the claim, begin its investigation, and request the information it needs.</li>
<li><strong>Within 15 business days after receiving everything it asked for,</strong> it generally must accept or reject the claim, or explain why it needs more time (up to 45 more days).</li>
<li><strong>Within 5 business days after accepting,</strong> it generally must pay.</li>
</ol>
<p>After a weather-related catastrophe declared by the commissioner, these deadlines can be extended by 15 days. TWIA claims follow Chapter 2210 instead (see our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page), and surplus lines carriers differ. If your claim has gone quiet past these windows, our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> page explains options.</p>

[[cta-storm]]

<h2>Contractors, Storm Chasers, and Your Claim</h2>
<p>After a big storm, out-of-town roofers and restoration crews arrive quickly. Many do good work, but be careful with anyone who:</p>
<ul>
<li>Asks you to sign a contract that assigns your claim or gives them authority to negotiate with your insurer</li>
<li>Offers to waive or “cover” your deductible</li>
<li>Pushes you to start permanent repairs before the insurer has inspected</li>
<li>Has no local permit history or cannot explain TDI windstorm inspection requirements for Jefferson County</li>
</ul>
<p>In Texas, public adjusters are licensed by TDI under Insurance Code Chapter 4102, and a public adjuster may not take part in the repairs on a claim they adjust. That separation keeps the person documenting your claim independent of the person doing the work.</p>

<h2>Mistakes That Slow Down Storm Claims</h2>
<p>Most storm claims that stall do so for avoidable reasons. Watch for these:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reporting late.</strong> Waiting until a leak gets worse, or until the next storm, makes it harder to tie damage to a date and can run into policy notice requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Throwing out evidence.</strong> Wet carpet, broken shingles, and spoiled contents should be photographed, and samples kept where practical, before they go to the curb.</li>
<li><strong>Mixing two storms together.</strong> If a spring hail storm and a summer tropical storm both hit the property, keep the photos, dates, and repairs separate.</li>
<li><strong>Not answering information requests.</strong> Under Chapter 542, many decision deadlines start when the insurer receives the items it asked for. Respond in writing and keep copies.</li>
<li><strong>Skipping temporary repairs.</strong> Policies generally require reasonable steps to protect the property. Damage that spreads because a roof was left open may not be paid.</li>
<li><strong>Assuming the first estimate is final.</strong> Insurers often issue an initial estimate quickly after a catastrophe. Hidden damage found later can be submitted with documentation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help After a Storm</h2>
<p>A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder. After a storm, that can include inspecting the full property, sorting damage by cause and coverage, preparing a line-item estimate, building a contents list, tracking insurer deadlines, and responding to requests for information. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster can also review an estimate you already received to see whether items such as permits, code items, or other structures were left out. Port Arthur Public Adjuster is a service of [[company]], TDI license #[[license]]. No result is guaranteed. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>For specific storm types, see our pages on <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage</a>, <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage</a>, and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage</a> claims.</p>
